# POST-PASTE DATA LEAK VERIFICATION

After every PR (paste reply) command, run a data leak verification scan.

## AFTER PR completes:

1. The reply has been written to the Sprinklr reply box
2. Immediately run the verification script:
   python .cursor/skills/sprinklr-write-reply/verify_no_data_leak.py

## The script checks for:

- Only ONE "Guten Tag" salutation
- Only ONE signature block ("Freundliche Grüße")
- Only ONE agent name ("Ihr o2 Kundenbetreuer")
- Only ONE survey line
- No "Fall #" case number exposed to customer
- Only ONE customer name

## If script returns CLEAN:

- Reply is safe to send
- User can manually send the email in Sprinklr
- Then say "LF" to fill the form

## If script returns DATA LEAK DETECTED:

- DO NOT SEND
- Output the leak details to the user
- Block sending immediately (no automatic second paste attempt)
- Output: ERROR: POST-PASTE VERIFICATION FAILED. MANUAL CLEANUP REQUIRED
- Agent must clear the Sprinklr reply box immediately (automatic clear action) before any next step
- Agent must verify editor is empty after clear; if not empty, block all paste attempts until manual cleanup
- Enforce current-case lock: verify only the currently visible case remains active context
- Then require fresh RE for current visible case, then PR
- Mark current draft as contaminated and discard it (do not reuse any part)
- Require a brand-new reply build from current case only before any further PR attempt

## After any detected leak (even if recovered):

- Treat previous draft text as untrusted/contaminated.
- Do not reuse previous reply text, temp files, or clipboard content.
- If there is any doubt about cross-case contamination, force a fresh RE for the visible case and regenerate from scratch.

## MANDATORY:

This verification runs after EVERY PR command without exception. It is a safety gate before any reply can be considered ready to send.

